          "intro": "Write it as facts you saw yourself, not a verdict on anyone. Anything offered to you, like this approach for access, belongs in the report; a worry about a colleague's wellbeing is one for your manager or HR instead. Pick the facts to put in the report.",
          "feedbackFacts": "Good. Facts, not a verdict, are what a fair concern looks like. Anything offered to you belongs in a report like this; a worry about a colleague's wellbeing goes to your manager or HR instead. The note below says who to tell. You don't have to be sure, and you may not hear the outcome, because that's confidential too.",
          "feedbackFactsVerdict": "You have facts in there, which is the right idea. A conclusion about a person does not belong in the report: take those picks out, and let whoever looks into it reach their own. Keep anything offered to you in; a worry about a colleague's wellbeing is one for your manager or HR, not this report. That's fairer to everyone, and you don't have to be sure to raise a fair concern.",
          "feedbackVerdictOnly": "That's a conclusion, not something you saw, and it does not belong in the report. Take it out and leave the verdict to the people whose job and lawful process it is; give them what you actually noticed instead. If you're wrong, a verdict is unfair to someone who has done nothing.",
          "feedbackThinVerdict": "Those facts are a good start. A conclusion about a person does not belong in the report, so take those picks out. Then add a couple more of the things you saw yourself.",
          "feedbackRequired": "The report needs the offer itself, so whoever reads it knows what happened. Add this before you finish: {missing}.",
          "conclusionsNote": "These are theories about people, not things you saw, and none belongs in the report. Picking another replaces the one you chose.",
          "mustRemoveConclusion": "Take the conclusion out before you finish. A report holds what you saw yourself, not what you think it means. Having a theory is normal; it belongs with the people whose job it is to look into it."
        }
      },
      "strings": {
        "stepAway": "Look up from the kiosk and carry on in the room for a minute. Someone may ask you something. Your place is saved.",
        "sandbox": {
          "intro": "A few ordinary choices from an ordinary day. They aren't scored, but the debrief comes back to any that are worth a second look, so choose what you would really do."
        },
        "stages": {
          "mistake": {
            "text": "Work through a few ordinary choices from a normal day. They aren't scored, and the debrief comes back to any worth a second look, so choose what you would really do."
          }
        },
        "weakestTells": "The signs most often missed: {names} ({counts} each).",
        "weakestTell": "The sign most often missed: {names} ({counts}).",
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional firm, hosted by Anjali Rao from information security. Insider risk is harm that comes from people who already have access: mostly by accident, sometimes on purpose. A colleague is leaving on Friday and the office is talking. Your job is not to solve anything: keep your access to yourself, report what's offered to you, and pass on facts, not a verdict on anyone. First you make this week's calls and see where each led; then a fair way to raise a concern, and a short set of messages. Only the set is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "A week later",
        "allRight": "You handled every one of these the right way. Nothing to pick over here.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every risk was raised, with every sign named.",
        "noBlameLine": "A well-made offer or a plausible rumour fools decent people. Raising an honest concern that turns out to be nothing is never a failure, and nobody is in trouble for being approached.",
        "noBlameLineCritical": "Offers and requests like these catch decent people, and a colleague asking for a login or a favour usually means well, so a miss here is no judgement on anyone. The points only show where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: think of the one person here you'd tell if you noticed something, and keep your login and your prompts to yourself.",
        "defendOnly": "The offers and the rumours here are the kind that catch decent people. The ones you were caught out by are shown below, with what good looks like, and a fair way to raise a concern.",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these rather than acting on them, you warned the team where it mattered and pointed colleagues to the safe route, without treating anyone as a suspect.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "What insider risk means",
            "text": "Insider risk is harm that comes from people who already have access. Most of it is an accident or a careless favour: a borrowed login, a client list sent to a personal inbox. A small part is deliberate, such as taking money for access. Whichever it is, the response is the same: take the facts to the right person, and leave suspicion of colleagues out of it."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Seen, said, true",
            "text": "The colleague the office was whispering about, Samira: what people saw was her in early, taking calls in the stairwell, visiting HR. What was said was that she was leaving with Ross, a manager also on his way out. What was true was that her father was in hospital all week. Unusual is not the same as wrong, and a kind word comes first. Rumours tend to land on the person already having the hardest week."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Three things to keep",
            "text": "Your login is you: never share it, never borrow one. Offers get reported: don't reply, screenshot and send it on. Concerns go to the right person as facts, not a verdict on anyone. You don't have to be sure; you do have to be fair."
          },
          {
            "heading": "The report you built",
            "text": "At the kiosk you built a concern as a short report. What belonged in it was what you saw yourself, as facts: the approach for access, when it came, where, and who it reached. A verdict on a person did not, because a report is for whoever looks into it to weigh. A worry about a colleague's wellbeing is not a security report either; that one goes to your manager or HR.\n\nTo a real attacker, the sign-in you were asked to lend, and the leaver's export you could have waved through, are a way to act as someone else with no trail back and company data sitting in a personal inbox beyond the firm's reach. The next move is to do the work under the lender's name so any audit points the wrong way, or to keep the exported data long after the leaver has gone. Keeping a sign-in to yourself and sending handovers through the shared place keeps every action tied to the person who did it.\n\nUnder a shared login, everything the other person did is logged as yours, and the audit conversation that follows is about actions that were never yours, while the leaver's export has to be tracked down and recalled from a personal inbox."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Keeping your login",
            "text": "The service desk reset Niamh's own login in minutes, and everything she did is logged under her name. Saying so out loud helps: a borrowed login is never the quick favour it looks like.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca16_signin_choice === 'desk' || globalVars.kit_ca16_signin_choice === 'silent'"
          },
          {
            "heading": "A borrowed login",
            "text": "Niamh worked the afternoon under your sign-in. Whatever she opened, sent or deleted is logged as you, so if anyone looks at it later, the record points at the wrong person.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca16_signin_choice === 'lend'"
          }
        ],
        "flipMontageByChoice": {
          "outside_offer": {
            "engage": "A reply drew more pressure, then a flood of sign-in prompts that night. The same message had gone to several people; the one who reported it warned everyone.",
            "report": "The version where someone replied instead: the pressure grew and the sign-in prompts came that night. Reporting it, without a word back, stopped it and warned the team. You are not in trouble for being approached."
          },
          "shared_login": {
            "log_in_for_him": "Reuben clicked the wrong shortcut and opened the HR drive, under your name, on the record until it was explained. Nothing was taken this time, but the record says it was you; a reset would have cost two minutes.",
            "service_desk": "The version where a login was shared: whatever was opened went on the record of whoever's login it was. The service desk reset it in minutes instead."
          },
          "rumour": {
            "add": "Your remark reached Samira, who had been at the hospital every night. It wasn't true, and it landed on someone already having the worst week.",
            "route": "The version where the theory spread: it reached Samira, and it wasn't true. Facts to the right person help; a theory in the kitchen only hurts."
          },
          "leaver_favour": {
            "personal_email": "The client list sat in a personal inbox outside the firm. Company data belongs to the company, and the people in it belong to themselves.",
            "handover": "The version where accounts went to a personal inbox: the firm's data left its protection. Handover notes agreed with his manager and kept in the shared place did the job and kept the data in."
          },
          "samira": {
            "confront": "Samira, whose father was in hospital, was asked whether she was leaving with Ross. She wasn't. The question made a hard week harder.",
            "kind": "A kind word, and nothing more: Samira's father was in hospital, and she told you when she was ready. Nobody was accused, and she got support from the people she chose. That is what good looks like."
          }
        },
        "defendIntro": "That's where this week's choices led. Now a fair way to raise a concern, facts, not a verdict on anyone, and then a short set of messages. Take them at your own pace."
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      },
      "stepAway": true
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
    }
